**Sec. 70001.** This section establishes that references to the Internal Revenue Code of 1986 in this title are to that specific code unless otherwise stated. It also clarifies that Section 15 of the Internal Revenue Code, which governs the effect of rate changes, does not apply to any tax rate changes made by this title.

**Sec. 70101.** This section makes the reduced individual income tax rates from the Tax Cuts and Jobs Act permanent by removing the sunset date of January 1, 2026. It modifies the inflation adjustment rules so that only the thresholds for rate brackets higher than 12 percent and 22 percent are adjusted for inflation. The amendments apply to taxable years beginning after December 31, 2025.

**Sec. 70102.** This section permanently extends the increased standard deduction and raises the base amounts to $23,625 for single filers and $15,750 for married individuals filing separately. It adjusts the inflation indexing base year for these amounts to 2024. The changes apply to taxable years beginning after December 31, 2024.

**Sec. 70103.** This section permanently terminates the deduction for personal exemptions but introduces a temporary $6,000 deduction for taxpayers or spouses aged 65 or older for taxable years beginning before January 1, 2029. This senior deduction is phased out by 6 percent for every dollar of modified adjusted gross income exceeding $75,000 ($150,000 for joint filers). The provision requires the inclusion of a valid Social Security number and applies to taxable years beginning after December 31, 2024.

**Sec. 70104.** This section permanently extends the Child Tax Credit and increases the maximum credit per qualifying child from $2,000 to $2,200. It establishes inflation adjustments for both the $1,400 refundable portion and the $2,200 total credit amount, with rounding to the nearest $100. The amendments require a valid Social Security number for the child and apply to taxable years beginning after December 31, 2024.

**Sec. 70105.** This section permanently extends the Qualified Business Income (QBI) deduction and raises the taxable income phase-in thresholds from $50,000/$100,000 to $75,000/$150,000. It introduces a minimum QBI deduction of $400 for taxpayers with at least $1,000 in aggregate qualified business income from active trades or businesses. Both the $400 minimum and $1,000 threshold are subject to inflation adjustments starting after 2026, and the changes apply to taxable years beginning after December 31, 2025.

**Sec. 70106.** This section permanently increases the basic estate and gift tax exemption amount from $5,000,000 to $15,000,000, indexed for inflation based on 2025. It removes the previous sunset provision that would have reduced the exemption to the pre-2018 level. The amendments apply to estates of decedents dying and gifts made after December 31, 2025.

**Sec. 70107.** This section permanently extends the increased Alternative Minimum Tax (AMT) exemption amounts and modifies the phaseout thresholds. It changes the inflation adjustment base year for the $1,000,000 phaseout threshold to 2025 and increases the phaseout rate from 25 percent to 50 percent. The amendments apply to taxable years beginning after December 31, 2025.

**Sec. 70108.** This section permanently extends the limitation on the deduction for qualified residence interest, which generally limits deductible interest to that on the first $750,000 of acquisition debt. It adds a provision treating mortgage insurance premiums as interest for purposes of this limitation. The amendments apply to taxable years beginning after December 31, 2025.

**Sec. 70109.** This section permanently extends the limitation on casualty loss deductions, which generally requires losses to exceed 10 percent of adjusted gross income. It expands the definition of qualifying disasters to include "State declared disasters" determined by the Governor (or Mayor of D.C.) and the Secretary of the Treasury. The amendments apply to taxable years beginning after December 31, 2025.

**Sec. 70110.** This section permanently terminates the miscellaneous itemized deductions subject to the 2 percent floor, except for educator expenses. It expands the definition of educator expenses to include coaches and interscholastic sports administrators, as well as nonathletic instructional equipment. The amendments apply to taxable years beginning after December 31, 2025.

**Sec. 70111.** This section reinstates a limitation on the tax benefit of itemized deductions by reducing them by 3/37 of the lesser of the total itemized deductions or the amount by which taxable income exceeds the threshold for the 37 percent tax bracket. This limitation is applied after other deduction limits and does not affect the calculation of the Qualified Business Income deduction. The amendments apply to taxable years beginning after December 31, 2025.

**Sec. 70112.** This section permanently extends the qualified transportation fringe benefits, such as transit passes and parking, while removing the specific exclusion for bicycle commuting reimbursements from the definition of qualified benefits. It adjusts the inflation indexing base year for the monthly limit on these benefits to 1997. The amendments apply to taxable years beginning after December 31, 2025.

**Sec. 70113.** This section permanently extends the limitation on the deduction and exclusion for moving expenses, which generally disallows these deductions for most individuals. It creates an exception allowing members of the intelligence community (other than Armed Forces members) to deduct moving expenses incurred due to a change in assignment requiring relocation. The amendments apply to taxable years beginning after December 31, 2025.

**Sec. 70114.** This section modifies the deduction for wagering losses by allowing a deduction equal to 90 percent of such losses, rather than 100 percent. The deduction remains limited to the amount of gains from wagering transactions during the same taxable year. The amendments apply to taxable years beginning after December 31, 2025.

**Sec. 70115.** This section permanently extends the increased annual contribution limit for ABLE accounts, which is tied to the annual gift tax exclusion amount. It adjusts the inflation indexing base year for this limit to 1996. The amendments apply to contributions made after December 31, 2025.

**Sec. 70116.** This section permanently extends the Saver’s Credit for contributions to ABLE accounts and increases the maximum credit amount from $2,000 to $2,100. It repeals a specific provision from the SECURE 2.0 Act of 2022 regarding the definition of qualified retirement savings contributions. The credit increase applies to taxable years beginning after December 31, 2026, while the extension applies to taxable years ending after December 31, 2025.

**Sec. 70117.** This section permanently extends the provision allowing tax-free rollovers from qualified tuition programs (529 plans) to ABLE accounts. The amendment removes the sunset date of January 1, 2026. The changes apply to taxable years beginning after December 31, 2025.

**Sec. 70118.** This section makes the tax exclusion for income earned by individuals performing services in the Sinai Peninsula permanent. It expands the list of qualified hazardous duty areas to include Kenya, Mali, Burkina Faso, and Chad, provided U.S. Armed Forces members are entitled to special pay for hostile fire or imminent danger in those locations. The amendments take effect on January 1, 2026.

**Sec. 70119.** This section permanently extends the exclusion from gross income for student loans discharged on account of the borrower's death or total and permanent disability. It applies to both federal student loans and private education loans and requires the taxpayer to include their Social Security number on the return. The amendments apply to discharges after December 31, 2025.

**Sec. 70120.** This section permanently extends the State and Local Tax (SALT) deduction cap but significantly increases the limit to $40,000 for 2025 and $40,400 for 2026, with 1 percent annual increases until 2029. The cap phases down to $10,000 for taxable years beginning after 2029 and is reduced by 30 percent of modified adjusted gross income exceeding $500,000 ($250,000 for separate filers), with a floor of $10,000. The amendments apply to taxable years beginning after December 31, 2024.

**Sec. 70201.** This section creates a new deduction for "qualified tips" received by individuals in occupations that customarily received tips before December 31, 2024, capped at $25,000 per year. The deduction is phased out by $100 for every $1,000 of modified adjusted gross income exceeding $150,000 ($300,000 for joint filers) and is available to non-itemizers. It excludes tips from specified service trades or businesses and terminates for taxable years beginning after December 31, 2028.

**Sec. 70202.** This section creates a new deduction for "qualified overtime compensation" paid under the Fair Labor Standards Act, capped at $12,500 for single filers and $25,000 for joint filers. The deduction is phased out by $100 for every $1,000 of modified adjusted gross income exceeding $150,000 ($300,000 for joint filers) and is available to non-itemizers. It excludes qualified tips and terminates for taxable years beginning after December 31, 2028.

**Sec. 70203.** This section allows a deduction for interest paid on loans for the purchase of applicable passenger vehicles manufactured primarily in the United States, capped at $10,000 per year. The deduction is phased out by $200 for every $1,000 of modified adjusted gross income exceeding $100,000 ($200,000 for joint filers) and is available to non-itemizers. It excludes loans for commercial vehicles, fleet sales, leases, salvage titles, or vehicles not finally assembled in the U.S., and applies to indebtedness incurred after December 31, 2024.

**Sec. 70204.** This section establishes "Trump Accounts," which are individual retirement accounts for children under 18, with a $5,000 annual contribution limit (indexed for inflation) and investments limited to low-cost index funds. It allows employer contributions up to $2,500 per employee (indexed for inflation) and creates a pilot program providing a $1,000 payment to the Trump Account of each U.S. citizen child born between 2025 and 2028. The section appropriates $410 million to the Treasury to fund the pilot program and applies to taxable years beginning after December 31, 2025.

**Sec. 70301.** This section makes 100 percent bonus depreciation permanent for qualified property acquired after January 19, 2025. It allows a transitional election for the first taxable year ending after January 19, 2025, to use a reduced 40 percent or 60 percent depreciation rate. The provision also extends the eligibility for bonus depreciation to certain plants and self-constructed property.

**Sec. 70302.** This section allows for the immediate expensing of domestic research and experimental expenditures, while foreign research expenditures must be amortized over 15 years. It provides transition rules allowing small businesses to retroactively apply the expensing to years beginning after December 31, 2021, and allows other taxpayers to deduct unamortized amounts from 2022-2024. The changes apply to amounts paid or incurred in taxable years beginning after December 31, 2024.

**Sec. 70303.** This section modifies the business interest limitation by removing the temporary exception for taxable years beginning before January 1, 2022. It expands the definition of floor plan financing to include trailers and campers designed for temporary living quarters. The amendments apply to taxable years beginning after December 31, 2024.

**Sec. 70304.** This section enhances the Paid Family and Medical Leave Credit by allowing employers to claim the credit based on insurance premiums paid for leave coverage, even if no leave was taken. It modifies the aggregation rules for related employers and excludes state-mandated leave from the credit calculation. The amendments apply to taxable years beginning after December 31, 2025.

**Sec. 70305.** This section restores the 50 percent deduction for business meals, except for certain entertainment expenses. It specifically exempts meals provided on fishing vessels or at fish processing facilities located north of 50 degrees north latitude and outside metropolitan statistical areas from the 50 percent limitation. The amendments apply to amounts paid or incurred after December 31, 2025.

**Sec. 70306.** This section increases the Section 179 expensing limit for depreciable business assets from $1,000,000 to $2,500,000. It also increases the phase-out threshold from $2,500,000 to $4,000,000. The amendments apply to property placed in service in taxable years beginning after December 31, 2024.

**Sec. 70307.** This section creates a special 100 percent depreciation allowance for qualified production property, defined as nonresidential real property used in manufacturing, production, or refining. The property must be placed in service between 2025 and 2030, and the deduction is subject to recapture if the property ceases to be used for qualified production activities within 10 years. The amendments apply to property placed in service after the date of enactment.

**Sec. 70308.** This section increases the Advanced Manufacturing Investment Credit rate from 25 percent to 35 percent. The amendment applies to property placed in service after December 31, 2025.

**Sec. 70309.** This section treats spaceports like airports for purposes of exempt facility bond rules, allowing them to issue tax-exempt bonds. It defines spaceports as facilities near launch or reentry sites used for manufacturing, flight control, or launch services, and exempts them from certain public use requirements. The amendments apply to obligations issued after the date of enactment.

**Sec. 70311.** This section modifies the foreign tax credit limitation by allocating specific deductions, such as the Section 250 deduction, to foreign source net CFC tested income. It prevents the allocation of interest expense and research expenditures to this income. The amendments apply to taxable years beginning after December 31, 2025.

**Sec. 70312.** This section increases the deemed paid foreign tax credit for taxes attributable to tested income from 80 percent to 90 percent. It disallows a foreign tax credit for 10 percent of foreign taxes paid on amounts excluded from gross income under Section 959(a) due to Section 951A inclusions. The credit increase applies to taxable years beginning after December 31, 2025, while the disallowance applies to taxes paid after June 28, 2025.

**Sec. 70313.** This section sources up to 50 percent of income from the sale of U.S.-produced inventory through foreign branches as foreign source income. This change affects the calculation of the foreign tax credit limitation. The amendment applies to taxable years beginning after December 31, 2025.

**Sec. 70321.** This section reduces the deduction rate for foreign-derived deduction eligible income from 37.5 percent to 33.34 percent. It also reduces the deduction rate for net CFC tested income from 50 percent to 40 percent. The amendments apply to taxable years beginning after December 31, 2025.

**Sec. 70322.** This section modifies the determination of deduction eligible income by including income from the sale of intangible property and depreciable property in the calculation. It limits expense apportionment to expenses properly allocable to such gross income, excluding interest and research expenditures. The sale/disposition rule applies to transactions after June 16, 2025, while the expense rule applies to taxable years beginning after December 31, 2025.

**Sec. 70323.** This section replaces the Global Intangible Low-Taxed Income (GILTI) regime with a "Net CFC Tested Income" regime, eliminating the tax-free deemed return on foreign investments. It renames Foreign-Derived Intangible Income (FDII) to Foreign-Derived Deduction Eligible Income (FDDEI). The amendments apply to taxable years beginning after December 31, 2025.

**Sec. 70331.** This section increases the Base Erosion Minimum Tax (BEMT) rate from 10 percent to 10.5 percent. It removes the reduced 13 percent rate for certain financial services companies. The amendments apply to taxable years beginning after December 31, 2025.

**Sec. 70341.** This section coordinates the business interest limitation with interest capitalization provisions, requiring the limitation to apply to both deducted and capitalized interest. It specifies that the allowed interest amount is applied first to capitalized interest. The amendments apply to taxable years beginning after December 31, 2025.

**Sec. 70342.** This section modifies the definition of adjusted taxable income for the business interest limitation to include amounts included in gross income under Sections 951(a), 951A(a), and 78. It also includes the corresponding deductions under Sections 245A(a) and 250(a)(1)(B). The amendments apply to taxable years beginning after December 31, 2025.

**Sec. 70351.** This section permanently extends the look-thru rule for related controlled foreign corporations, which allows certain income to be treated as if it were earned by the U.S. shareholder. The amendment removes the sunset date of January 1, 2026. It applies to taxable years of foreign corporations beginning after December 31, 2025.

**Sec. 70352.** This section repeals the election for a one-month deferral in determining the taxable year of specified foreign corporations. It requires these corporations to align their taxable year with the first required year ending after November 30, 2025. The amendments apply to taxable years beginning after November 30, 2025.

**Sec. 70353.** This section restores the limitation on downward attribution of stock ownership in constructive ownership rules, preventing U.S. persons from being treated as owning stock held by non-U.S. persons. It creates a new Section 951B to include amounts in the gross income of foreign controlled U.S. shareholders of foreign controlled foreign corporations. The amendments apply to taxable years of foreign corporations beginning after December 31, 2025.

**Sec. 70354.** This section modifies the pro rata share rules for Subpart F income and Section 956 inclusions, requiring inclusion based on the period during which the shareholder owned the stock and the corporation was a CFC. It coordinates these rules with Section 951A and provides transition rules for dividends. The amendments apply to taxable years of foreign corporations beginning after December 31, 2025. [end of text]


